Close the three races where two turns on one session meet

Consolidation was read-then-act: read a batch, distill it, then mark it. Two
concurrent turns both saw "due" and distilled the same log twice, burning a model
call and bumping consolidation_tries twice. It is now one `UPDATE … RETURNING`
that selects and marks together, so the loser gets an empty list and never calls
the model. Because claiming happens before processing, every failure path
releases the claim (consolidated back to 0 plus the existing exponential
backoff) — a claim that is never returned would strand those rows as "distilled"
with nothing written, which is real data loss, not a race.

The rolling summary's upsert took whichever write landed last, so concurrent
compaction could hand the user an older summary with nothing on screen to say so.
It now only accepts a summary covering more turns, and says so on stderr when it
refuses.

The busy_timeout story was worse than a race: the comment said 3 seconds and the
real value was sqlx's default 5, because `PRAGMA busy_timeout` is per-connection
and `execute(&pool)` only configures whichever connection served that query. The
parameters now live on SqliteConnectOptions, so every connection carries them,
and a test reads the PRAGMAs back — it caught the 5-vs-3 lie on the first run.

Background job outbox filenames carry the pid: job ids are per-process, so two
Joy processes wrote the same j1.txt.

No per-session lock, deliberately. Holding one during a turn's write section
would make the second turn visibly slower, and the three guards already reduce
what concurrency can do to "waits a bit longer"; limitations now says so in both
languages instead of leaving it as folklore.

Tests: two concurrent consolidations assert the small model is called once (and
that the loser sees no script), a failed consolidation releases its claim, a
rollup only moves forward (new not overwritten by old, old not overwriting new,
equal not an update), the PRAGMAs are really in effect, and the outbox file is
named with the pid and holds the ring's tail. 321 Rust tests, docs: limitations
(new Concurrency section), internals 2, testing, CHANGELOG — EN + zh.
